WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:13.050
I grew up in the suburbs of Chicago,
which was a wonderful liberal bubble.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.490
Then I moved to Tennessee for college.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.300
After that, I moved to Texas for
my first job.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.170
I met people who grew up shooting
guns with their grandfathers.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:30.049
People who had strong religious
convictions about abortion and

00:00:30.049 --> 00:00:30.990
gay marriage.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:39.915
If I'm being totally honest,
the whole thing stressed me out.

00:00:39.915 --> 00:00:42.920
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I knew we disagreed about so

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.340
many things, but
I didn't want to bring them up.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:53.120
I was worried I might offend someone,
that I might get labeled as intense.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.100
Or that I might end up in one
of those politically correct,

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.849
agree to disagree situations
that everyone hates.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.530
And I don't think I'm alone.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:09.470
For most of us, talking about politics
is stressful and awkward, and

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.210
if it's not it's probably because you're
only talking to people who agree with you.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.850
So, what I want to do today is
talk about talking about politics.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:27.750
Why we don't, why we should and
most importantly, how we can do it better.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.270
So, why don't we like
talking about politics?

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.270
For one thing,

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:38.330
we're taught from a very young age
that the topic is just rude, right?

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:43.467
Just like money or religion,
I'm not supposed to know your salary,

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:47.559
question your belief in God,
or ask who you voted for.

00:01:49.735 --> 00:01:52.332
And then even when we do
start to talk about politics,

00:01:52.332 --> 00:01:55.400
we usually don't think we're
going to change anybody's mind.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.420
It's not that often that we convince
somebody to change a really

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.750
deep seated political view.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.420
Even more fundamentally,
when we talk about politics,

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.190
we often feel insecure and defensive.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.820
Have you ever worried that someone
might think you're uninformed?

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.010
That you might get judge for
a controversial opinion?

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.240
Research shows that when people are shown
information that contradicts their

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.140
political views, the parts of their brain
that light up are those associated with

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:35.720
personal identity,
emotion, threat response.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:41.528
In other words, we feel insecure and
defensive because we're literally wired

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:46.940
to view attacks on our political
beliefs as attacks on ourselves.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.082
So we're conditioned to
believe the topic is rude,

00:02:52.082 --> 00:02:54.755
we don't think we're going to
change anybody's mind.

00:02:54.755 --> 00:02:56.960
And we feel insecure and defensive.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.910
So then why should we talk about politics,

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.800
isn't it easier to just leave
it to the talking heads on CNN.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.190
I actually think that talking about
politics sharpens our thinking.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:16.120
When I spoke to my conservative friends in
Tennessee and Texas, I realized that a lot

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.835
of things I think the government should
do actually cost a whole lot of money.

00:03:21.835 --> 00:03:25.800
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sharing, I believe,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:30.230
helps us challenge, crystallize,
reassess what we believe.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.050
We start recognizing trade-offs and
our views become more nuanced.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:42.240
And hey, if anyone is going to change your
mind about something, it's probably not

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.735
going to be Anderson Cooper, despite
that persuasive jawline I wish I had.

00:03:47.735 --> 00:03:50.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's going to be your friends,

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:51.990
neighbors, coworkers,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:56.400
classmates, people you trust and that you
can have an actual conversation with.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:03:59.670
Even more importantly though,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.740
talking about politics helps us
share ourselves with the world.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.050
We aren't born with any
set of political beliefs.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:14.500
The issues we care most about come
from out most meaningful experiences.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.620
For me, I think about the day a close
friend in high school came out to me.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.355
That was the day that gay
rights became personal.

00:04:28.391 --> 00:04:33.463
I think about the incredible public school
teachers who are literally the reason I am

00:04:33.463 --> 00:04:39.410
at Stanford today, Why I care so
much about investing in public education.

00:04:42.643 --> 00:04:48.037
I think about my parents,
who came to this country

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:52.661
from India with nothing in their pockets,

00:04:52.661 --> 00:04:58.070
to give their children,
to give me a better life.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:06.680
That's why I think immigrants
should be celebrated, not vilified.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:16.204
This is the stuff that makes you, you.

00:05:16.204 --> 00:05:21.450
And the real you, the you that is human
in the most beautiful and authentic and

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:26.961
wonderful of ways it's too valuable and
too precious to leave locked up inside.

00:05:30.491 --> 00:05:34.190
So we need to talk about politics more and
we need to talk about politics better.

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:39.300
And so, here are the five most practical
things I can tell about how to do that.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:47.156
First, come from a place of
curiosity not offense or defense.

00:05:47.156 --> 00:05:52.036
I know that sounds like Tom Brady
started reading fortune cookies so,

00:05:52.036 --> 00:05:53.950
let me give you an example.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.512
When I was in college,

00:05:56.512 --> 00:06:01.511
I moderated a debate between the college
Democrats in the college Republicans.

00:06:01.511 --> 00:06:06.048
It actually went really well and so
naturally I was walking around afterwards

00:06:06.048 --> 00:06:09.910
being like move over, Tom Brokaw,
because there is a new face at NBC.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.720
But the woman who was organizing
the debate ran up to me furious.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:22.960
She made me watch the video, when the
Democrat was speaking I was nodding and

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.815
smiling uncontrollably.

00:06:24.815 --> 00:06:28.495
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I literally looked like a bobble head.

00:06:28.495 --> 00:06:31.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; When the Republican was speaking,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:34.525
I just looked angry.

00:06:34.525 --> 00:06:37.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was not coming from a place of

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.900
curiosity.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.639
I was coming from a place of offense and
defense.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.950
I just wanted my team to win and

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:49.250
as a result, missed out on
the opportunity to learn something.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.550
Second, pick the right time and place.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.100
Discussions about politics can
be really difficult when there's

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:59.810
an audience watching.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:02.920
Whether that's a real audience
around a dinner table, or

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.630
a virtual audience on Facebook.

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:06.440
Try waiting for

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.660
more intimate opportunity to talk to
someone like over a cup of coffee.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.820
As one mediation expert puts it,
don't always stand up

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.660
to those with different beliefs,
instead try sitting down with them.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:25.190
Three, focus on one topic at a time.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:28.720
Don't attack the entire Democratic or

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.020
Republican Party or everything the person
has believed in their entire lives.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.680
That's when we feel most defensive and
insecure.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.490
By focusing on one topic,

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.317
we can actually accomplish something over
the course of a single conversation.

00:07:42.317 --> 00:07:50.810
Fourth, listen actively, use body language
that shows you're paying attention.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:55.310
Ask questions, paraphrase to verify you
understand what their talking about.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.601
Most people need to feel
like they've been heard,

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:03.250
before they'll listen to someone else,
or consider changing their mind.

00:08:05.338 --> 00:08:12.665
Fifth and finally, avoid verbal attacks
and obviously don't hit anybody.

00:08:12.665 --> 00:08:15.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Even if we're not usually

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:20.790
ones to lose control, it's shocking
how quickly our language can change

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:23.467
when we're talking about
things we really care about.

00:08:23.467 --> 00:08:28.146
Language that's zero-sum or
that frames things in

00:08:28.146 --> 00:08:32.943
terms of good and
evil can kill productive dialogue.

00:08:36.152 --> 00:08:42.430
So, let's review, come from a place of
curiosity, pick the right time and place.

00:08:42.430 --> 00:08:48.860
Focus on one topic at a time,
listen actively, and avoid attacks.

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:52.740
I know that's actually a lot to remember.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:58.080
I don't have a cute acronym for
you to take home and hold your gasps,

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.220
but I broke the magic rule of three.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:06.340
But that's kind of the point,
of course, it's not going to be easy.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:11.900
We're talking about unlearning what
we've been taught our entire lives,

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:13.630
rewiring a part of our brain.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.588
And just like always,

00:09:16.588 --> 00:09:22.532
talking about politics is sometimes going
to be stressful, awkward, you name it.

00:09:22.532 --> 00:09:24.794
But I'll tell you something,

00:09:24.794 --> 00:09:29.318
the conversations I had over six
years of living in the South,

00:09:29.318 --> 00:09:34.540
have made me a better friend,
a better leader, and a better American.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:41.820
So let's stop taking the easy way out,
let's embrace discomfort

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:49.353
and let's start talking about the things
that really matter, thank you.

00:09:49.353 --> 00:09:50.652
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:50.652 --> 00:09:55.003
[MUSIC]

